   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Scaling up HIV prevention among Most at Risk Population in Malaysia - Malaysia (01-Jul-2016-31-Dec-2018)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Malaysia, an estimated 105, 400 people are living with HIV and the estimated number of new infections annually stands at 10, 375. Being the population with the highest HIV prevalence, IDU-related interventions are the primary target of this program. Interventions for female and mak nyah (transgendered) sex workers have been chosen as these populations have the second highest prevalence. The activities under this program support the Malaysia National Strategic Plan (NSP) on HIV/AIDS from 2011 to 2015, which aims to prevent the transmission of HIV among IDUs, sex workers, mak nyah, and men who have sex with men. These include outreach strategies for high risk groups and sensitization workshops targeted at law enforcement officers and other key community leaders. The current grant aims to increase the coverage of a core set of prevention activities by local nongovernmental organizations from 10 percent to 22 percent among IDUs and from 6 percent to 15 percent among female and mak nyah sex workers.</narrative>
  </description>
